# Cursor Rules — temple-era monorepo

## CRITICAL: Read AGENTS.md first

**MANDATORY — ALWAYS APPLY:**

1. **Before exploring the codebase**, read `AGENTS.md` at the repo root. It is the
   primary reference for workspace layout, tooling, and cross-app constraints.
   Then read the app-level file for whichever app you are working in:
   - `apps/web/AGENTS.md` — Next.js app: architecture, tRPC/Drizzle, API surface
   - `apps/bot/AGENTS.md` — Discord bot: handlers, gateway patterns

   `CLAUDE.md` files are one-line pointers at the `AGENTS.md` beside them; never
   edit a `CLAUDE.md`.

2. **When making repository updates**, update the AGENTS.md at the right level:
   - Workspace layout, root tooling, cross-app contracts → root `AGENTS.md`
   - Architecture, structure, commands, schema, endpoints, patterns for one app
     → that app's `AGENTS.md`
   Keep them synchronized with the actual codebase state.

3. **These rules apply to ALL interactions** — no exceptions.

## Monorepo awareness

This repo holds two independently-deployed apps. Before changing anything, know which
one you are in:

| Path | App | Deploys to |
|---|---|---|
| `apps/web` | Next.js 15 web app (owns the DB and all API surfaces) | Vercel |
| `apps/bot` | Discord gateway bot (thin client over `/api/discord/*`) | Northflank |

- Run commands from the **repo root** — Turborepo fans out to both apps.
  Target one with `pnpm --filter <name> <script>`.
- Changing an `/api/discord/*` endpoint in `apps/web` is a **contract change** the bot
  depends on. Update both sides in the same PR.
- `/api/v1/*`, `/api/discord/proxy/*`, and the OpenAPI spec are consumed by an external
  bot (Templar) that is not in this repo. **Never change their wire formats.**

## PR Generation Rules

### 1. Always return clickable links
- `[PR #123: Title](https://github.com/khlav/temple-era/pull/123)`
- Use full GitHub URLs for maximum compatibility

### 2. Branch management
**CRITICAL**: All work MUST be on non-main branches. Never commit directly to `main`.

- Verify the working tree with `git status` before starting
- If on `main`, create a branch; if already on a feature branch, use it
- Always branch, no matter how small the change

Branch format `{type}/{kebab-case-description}`, type one of:
`feature`, `fix`, `chore`, `refactor`, `hotfix`, `dev`, `claude`

### 3. "Ship it"
When the user says "ship it", follow `.claude/commands/ship.md` exactly. Do not
substitute your own shipping logic.

Commit format `{type}({scope}): {description}` — scope the message to the app touched:

- `apps/web/**` only → `feat(raids): add attendance export`
- `apps/bot/**` only → `fix(bot/handler): resolve thread parsing`
- Both, or root config → `chore(repo): bump turbo`

### 4. PR descriptions
- _Italics_ for inline code/file references instead of backticks
- 2–4 bullets max per section; omit empty sections
- User-focused — what people will see, not implementation detail
- Do not restate the title
- Follow `.github/pull_request_template.md`

### 5. `user-facing` label
Controls Discord notifications for merged PRs.

- Apply for `feature/` and `fix/` branches
- Skip for `chore/`, `dev/`, `refactor/`
- Skip for config-only changes with no user-visible effect
- `apps/bot/**`-only changes are rarely user-facing — the bot has no UI. Label only
  when guild members will notice a behaviour change in Discord.
